Frank tried to remember when he'd last felt such relief. It wasn't an emotion he recognized, or if it was, it was one he hadn't felt in many years. It was the way he used to feel when he saw the finish line ahead of him and had enough in the tank to sprint the last hundred yards, gathering up his sneakers and socks from the grass where he'd fallen in practice and crossing the line feeling lean and tough and limber. He might have been nine or ten. Or younger.

For a long time, he'd hated that memory. It belonged to the version of himself that he'd spent his adult life repudiating.

